\description{
Runs an analog impact or regression analysis in cross-validation mode,
generating held-out predictions and residuals for each location in \code{pool}.
Each location is predicted using only neighbors that exclude itself,
providing an honest assessment of how well the specified configuration
predicts observed \code{y} values. Supports leave-one-out (LOO) and k-fold
cross-validation methods.
}
\details{
\code{analog_cv()} supports two CV methods:
\itemize{
\item \code{"loo"} (leave-one-out): Each focal location excludes its own
pool row from its neighborhood. Implemented as a single call with
self-exclusion. Fast and the most granular form of CV.
\item \code{"kfold"}: Pool is partitioned into \code{n_folds} folds (or user-
supplied \code{fold_id}). Each fold's locations are predicted using the
remaining folds as the pool. Implemented as \code{k} separate calls with
the index rebuilt per fold. Reduces optimism from spatial
autocorrelation by holding out larger contiguous sets of locations
(if folds are spatially blocked).
}

Plain LOO excludes only each focal's own pool row, so a focal can still
be predicted by its immediate geographic neighbors. When \code{y} and the
environment are spatially autocorrelated those near-neighbors are
effectively near-duplicates, and LOO error is optimistically low as a
result. To perform \emph{buffered} (spatially blocked) LOO, pass a geographic
kernel with a \code{min} distance, which excludes any candidate closer than
\code{min} to the focal in addition to the self row: for example
\code{geog = kernel(max = 500, min = 50)} keeps analogs in a 50-500 km annulus
around each focal. Choosing \code{min} on the order of the autocorrelation
range of the residuals gives a less biased estimate of predictive skill
than either plain LOO or unblocked k-fold. See \code{\link[=kernel]{kernel()}} for details.

Supported functions: \code{\link[=analog_impact]{analog_impact()}}, \code{\link[=analog_regression]{analog_regression()}}, and
\code{\link[=analog_search]{analog_search()}}. Other \verb{analog_*()} functions have no \code{y} input and
thus no prediction to validate.

When \code{fun = analog_search}, residuals are computed against:
\itemize{
\item the \code{weighted_mean} column if \code{stat} includes \code{"weighted_mean"} but not
\code{"regression"} or \code{"tabulate"};
\item fitted values from regression coefficients if \code{stat} includes
\code{"regression"} but not \code{"weighted_mean"} or \code{"tabulate"};
\item per-class weighted votes if \code{stat} includes \code{"tabulate"} (categorical
y; produces Brier score and primary-class label rather than a numeric
residual).
}

If \code{stat} includes more than one of these, the prediction target is
ambiguous and \code{analog_cv()} will error. If it includes none, residuals
are skipped and only the underlying search columns are returned.

For categorical CV (\code{stat = "tabulate"}), \code{y} must be a factor or
coercible-to-factor input (character, integer codes, single-layer
categorical SpatRaster). The output uses different residual-equivalent
columns: see \verb{@return} below.
